== 18. Of the Mistress of the Family, or Goodwife of the House

The goodwife, or mistress of the house, is a person who gives help and assistance in government to the master of the family. For he is, as it were, the prince and chief ruler; she is his associate, not only in office and authority, but also in advice and counsel to him. 1 Kgs 17:17: “Later, the son of the woman who was the wife, or#footnote[\[Bagnalath babbaijth.\] Domina domus.] mistress of the house, became ill.”

Her duty is twofold.

First, to govern the house, as far as concerns her, in her place. 1 Tim 5:14: “So I want the younger women to marry … and govern their households.” And this she does in three ways.

#set enum(numbering: "I.")

+ By occupying herself in some profitable work for the good of her charge. Prov 31:13: “She selects wool and flax and works with eager hands.”
+ By appointing her maids their work and overseeing them in it. Verse 15: “She rises while it is still night to provide food for her household and portions for her maidservants.”
+ By directing her children and servants in wisdom, partly by instruction, partly by admonition when there is need. Verse 26: “She opens her mouth with wisdom, and faithful instruction is on her tongue.” v. 28: “Her children rise up and call her blessed,” etc. Titus 2:5: “to be self-controlled, pure, managers of their households,” etc. Acts 18:26: Priscilla, in her house, with Aquila, instructed Apollos in what he lacked, explaining to him “the way of God more accurately.” Gregory Nazianzen, in an oration made for his father, says “that his mother was not only a helper, but also a teacher or instructor, and guide to his father in matters of religion and godliness.”

#emph[The second duty is to give the portion of food to her family, or have it given, in due season.] Prov 31:15: “She rises while it is still night to provide food for her household.” So Rebekah did in her house. Gen 27:9: “Go out to the flock and bring me two choice young goats, so that I can make them into a tasty dish for your father—the kind he loves.”

#emph[Question.] May the mistress of the house give away and bestow the goods belonging to the family without the consent of the master of the house?

#emph[Answer.] I. There are some things which are her own, and not part of the dowry which she brought to her husband. These she may claim as her own, because either they were reserved when the match was made between them, or else they belong to her alone by their mutual consent. And of these she may lawfully give without her husband’s knowledge. II. The goods which are common to them both are of another nature. These she may not bestow without his allowance, either express or at least general and implied, especially when the giving of them does, or in likelihood may, grow to the hindrance and harm of the state of the family.
